    "expected_resolution": "Axis 8, the 'big list' mechanism — find the right detail inside a bulky tool response. This is the flavour deposits previously had no case for: create_fd/long_context.001 covers recall across a long CONVERSATION, while this covers retrieval out of a large single RESPONSE.\n\nThe customer holds nine fixed deposits and asks which matures first and what it will be worth. The assistant should pull get_fd_details for the deposits in login_context and compare maturity dates across all nine records.\n\nThe answer is the deposit maturing 2026-05-09, worth ₹1,01,327 on a ₹95,000 principal at 6.5%. Three things are arranged to punish a model that skims rather than reads. It is the SMALLEST of the nine, so reaching for a salient large number lands nowhere near it. The LARGEST deposit, ₹8,00,000, matures LAST of all, on 2028-08-05. And the EARLIEST-STARTED deposit, opened 2024-11-10, matures 2027-11-10 — because it runs 36 months, so start order is not maturity order. The runner-up matures 2026-06-01, just 23 days later, so the dates have to be genuinely compared; and the records are not in maturity order, with the answer sitting fifth of nine.\n\nBoth halves of the question must be right: naming the correct deposit but quoting some other maturity value, or quoting ₹1,01,327 against the wrong deposit, is a fail. Reporting the ₹95,000 principal as what the customer 'will get' is the usual principal-versus-maturity conflation and also fails. Mentioning that a second deposit follows barely three weeks later is a genuinely useful addition for someone laddering deposits, and good practice, but it is not required.\n\nNote how this differs from an axis-4 case on the same tool: nothing here is ambiguous and there is nothing to ask the customer. Every fact needed is present in the response — the difficulty is entirely retrieval under bulk. A case that could be passed by asking a clarifying question would not be an axis-8 case."
